.TH "DBFILE" 7 "September 2025" "Testserver Handlers" "Testserv Authors"

.SH "DBFILE"
.SH "NAME"
.PP
\fIdbfile\fP \- serve zone data from an RFC 1035\-style zone file

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
The \fIfile\fP plugin is used for an "old\-style" DNS server. It serves from a preloaded file that exists
on disk contained RFC 1035 styled data. If the zone file contains signatures (i.e., is signed using
DNSSEC), correct DNSSEC answers are returned, but only NSEC is supported. If you use this setup \fIyou\fP
are responsible for re\-signing the zonefile. See the \fIsign\fP plugin if you want to sign and resign your zone
though.

.SH "SYNTAX"
.PP
.RS

.nf
dbfile FILE

.fi
.RE

.IP \(bu 4
\fBFILE\fP the database file to read and parse. If the path is relative, the path from the global root config
will be prepended to it.


.PP
For extra control you can open the block are define multipe properties.

.PP
.RS

.nf
dbfile FILE {
    reload DURATION
    minimal disable
}

.fi
.RE

.IP \(bu 4
\fB\fCreload\fR will reload the zone every \fBDURATION\fP to check for SOA version changes. Default is one minute
(\fB\fC1m\fR). A value of \fB\fC0\fR means to not scan for changes. For example, \fB\fC30s\fR checks the zonefile every 30 seconds
and reloads the zone when serial changes.
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\fCminimal disable\fR makes \fIdbfile\fP return larger and more complete answers, the default is to omit the authoritative
section in positive answers. Almost all clients should be able to handle these kind of replies.
.IP \(bu 4
\fB\fCtransfer\fR ...


.SH "EXAMPLES"
.PP
Load the \fB\fCexample.org\fR zone from \fB\fCdb.example.org\fR and allow transfers to the internet, but send
notifies to 10.240.1.1

.PP
.RS

.nf
example.org {
    file db.example.org
    transfer {
        to * 10.240.1.1
    }
}

.fi
.RE

.PP
Where \fB\fCdb.example.org\fR would contain RRs in the (text) presentation format from RFC 1035:

.PP
.RS

.nf
$ORIGIN example.org.
@    3600 IN    SOA sns.dns.icann.org. noc.dns.icann.org. 2017042745 7200 3600 1209600 3600
    3600 IN NS a.iana\-servers.net.
    3600 IN NS b.iana\-servers.net.

www     IN A     127.0.0.1
        IN AAAA  ::1

.fi
.RE

.PP
Or use a single zone file for multiple zones:

.PP
.RS

.nf
example.org example.net {
    file example.org.signed {
        transfer example.org example.net {
            to * 10.240.1.1
        }
    }
}

.fi
.RE

.SH "SEE ALSO"
.PP
See the \fIsign\fP plugin for signing your zones and see RFC 1035
\[la]https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc1035.txt\[ra]
for more info on how to structure zone files.
